<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spanish flu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as 1918–1920 flu pandemic, Great Influenza epidemic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spanish flu was a global influenza pandemic of 1918–1920 caused by the H1N1 subtype of the influenza A virus. The earliest probable cases were documented in March 1918 in Haskell County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>; within two years an estimated 500 million people, nearly a third of the global population, had been infected, and estimates of deaths range from 17 million to 50 million, possibly as high as 100 million. The name is misleading: wartime censors suppressed news of the outbreak while newspapers in neutral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> reported it freely. A second wave from August 1918 was far deadlier than the first, the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Arnold estimating at least 12 million dead in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and a third wave followed in 1919.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spanish-flu.jpg" alt="Spanish flu" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otis Historical Archives, National Museum of Health and Medicine · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emergency_hospital_during_Influenza_epidemic,_Camp_Funston,_Kansas_-_NCP_1603.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The pandemic broke out near the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, when wartime censors in the belligerent countries suppressed bad news to maintain morale, while newspapers freely reported the outbreak in neutral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. This created a misleading impression of Spain as the epicentre, and historical and epidemiological data cannot identify the geographic origin. The close quarters and massive troop movements of the war hastened the spread and probably increased transmission and mutation. The basic reproduction number of the virus was between 2 and 3.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The pandemic is conventionally marked as beginning on 4 March 1918 with the recorded case of Albert Gitchell, an army cook at Camp Funston in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>, though there had been earlier cases. Within days 522 men at the camp had reported sick, and by 11 March the virus had reached Queens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Most of the infected in the first wave had only the typical symptoms of sore throat, headache and fever.</p><p>In the second wave the disease was much more serious, often complicated by bacterial pneumonia, which was often the cause of death. Victims developed heliotrope cyanosis, in which the face turned blue and then black, and death followed within hours or days as the lungs filled with fluid. Maritime quarantines saved many lives on islands such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and American Samoa, and social distancing measures included closing schools, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> and places of worship. With no antiviral drugs or antibiotics, doctors used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aspirin">aspirin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quinine">quinine</a>, castor oil and other remedies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The virus infected around 500 million people, about one-third of the world's population. Estimates of the dead vary greatly: 21.6 million in 1927, between 25 and 39 million in 1991, and 50 million, possibly as high as 100 million, in 2005; a 2018 reassessment gave about 17 million, though this has been contested. The pandemic killed mostly young adults: in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1918–1919, nearly half of deaths were in people aged 20 to 40.</p><p>Some communities escaped the worst. American Samoa and New Caledonia avoided deaths through quarantine, though the outbreak reached them later, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a> blocked the main road of the island to protect a third of its population. Bristol Bay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> had a death rate of 40 per cent. The historian Guy Beiner has shown that the pandemic, overshadowed by the commemoration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, was nevertheless remembered in private and local traditions across the globe.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spanish flu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
